WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.550
- [Narrator] On average,
flat roads like this

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.670
cost around $1 million
per lane mile to build.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.410
But these winding mountain roads

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.390
can cost up to 10 times that.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:10.290
That's because...

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.235
- You can't just excavate
rock. You have to blast.

00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:14.220
(rocks crashing)

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.960
- [Narrator] And that's really expensive,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.360
which is one of the reasons
why brand new mountain roads

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.890
aren't built very often.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.560
- We only wanna design
and build them once.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.210
- [Narrator] We asked
a structural engineer

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.130
with over 25 years of experience

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.152
to explain how these steep
inclines and sharp turns

00:00:29.152 --> 00:00:32.905
are designed to keep drivers
safe in the mountains.

00:00:32.905 --> 00:00:35.640
- There's a lot of different
kinds of mountain roads.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.460
I think as a tourist,
a lot of people think

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.000
of the very narrow, windy mountain roads.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.190
But additionally, you have interstates

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.980
going through the mountains
and those are mountain roads.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.510
- [Narrator] Heffron says
that digging a tunnel

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.570
through the mountain is usually
expensive and highly risky.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.330
So, managing that vertical climb is key.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.880
- That's what we call
the grade of the road,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.440
and we talk about it in percentages.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.810
So, for every hundred feet you travel,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.450
you wanna try to keep it to five feet max.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.283
In the mountains,

00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:06.450
a lot of times you're
going to exceed that.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.750
You may go 6.5 or even 10%.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.520
- [Narrator] Mountain
roads also have to maneuver

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.620
around a lot of natural obstacles,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.800
affecting its path or
horizontal alignment.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.270
- When you get to a mountain setting,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.020
the edges of your roadway
are defined by nature.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.700
One of the things you're trying to define

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.070
is how wide will the roadway be?

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.060
We may decide to narrow the
lane widths up to 11 feet

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.370
so that we can stay out of the creek,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.940
or not have to blast
into the adjacent rock.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.250
And these things are pretty
imperceptible to the driver.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.080
You don't really get a tape measure out

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.050
every a hundred feet, but
as a designer, you do.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.580
- [Narrator] When the
terrain gets too steep,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.290
switchbacks become necessary.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.565
Heffron says these tighter
turns also help reduce

00:01:48.565 --> 00:01:51.930
how much the mountain side
is affected by the road.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.470
- On a more rural low volume road,

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.280
you may have more switchbacks
and tighter turns.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.227
It's a nice 200 foot radius
to make that switchback

00:02:02.227 --> 00:02:03.930
and get around that curve.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.170
Whereas on an interstate,
instead of 200, you have 1,000.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.850
About five times that.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.050
- [Narrator] When the road curves,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.180
there's usually an added design feature

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.880
called superelevation,
which is where the roadway

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.850
is tilted to make it
easier to navigate a curve.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.850
- So, as you're entering
a curve as a driver,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.070
and you're turning,
the roadway isn't flat,

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.930
but it's going to super elevate,

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.060
it's going to raise on the high side.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.890
Kinda like you see at a speed track.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.580
The centripetal force will
keep you on the roadway.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.650
- [Narrator] Superelevation
is especially important

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.140
in the mountains where
roads get icy quickly.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.670
- If the roadway were flat,

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.650
especially if this roadway is icy,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.990
you could veer off the roadway.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.380
- [Narrator] Heffron says
superelevation starts at around 2%

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.530
and gets steeper as the speed
of the roadway increases.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.060
- Sometimes in the mountains
you'll go 5, 6%, maybe 8,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.970
but you're trying to keep it not too steep

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.109
because as this roadway ice is over,

00:02:59.109 --> 00:03:02.040
and if there's a lot of
traffic during a snowstorm

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.390
and the cars go slower,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.690
and they might have the
propensity to slide horizontally,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.640
which is not safe.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.470
- [Narrator] Rock fall
makes mountain road design

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:11.496
even more challenging.

00:03:11.496 --> 00:03:13.050
(rocks crashing)

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.480
- A lot of rock stability and rockfall

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.590
is controlled specifically
by the geometry.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.850
- [Narrator] This is Howard Hume.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.950
His job is to identify these.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.480
The breaks in the rock face,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.580
also called rock joints and...

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.740
- How they interact with the face angle.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.390
Whether they project outta the slope,

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.890
in which case they're a problem,

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.660
in which case you've gotta stop them.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.709
You can see horizontal
jointing in the face.

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:37.350
- [Narrator] Hume says the stability

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.060
of rock faces varies widely.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.760
But in general, if rock
joints are horizontal

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.370
or at a very low angle
with respect to the road...

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.420
- That's not a problem.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.850
If it's dipping steeply and
you're standing underneath it,

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.365
that's definitely a problem.

00:03:49.365 --> 00:03:51.150
- [Narrator] Hume and his team use a drone

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.280
to take hundreds or
even thousands of photos

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.330
of the rock face.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.553
- You got the area over there.

00:03:55.553 --> 00:03:58.166
- [Narrator] To create
models like this and this,

00:03:58.166 --> 00:04:01.290
which he uses to simulate
different types of rockfall.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:03.420
This helps him decide
the most stable angle

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:04.500
to cut the rock face.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.390
- When I say cut,
eventually rock like this,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.492
you've gotta blast it.

00:04:07.492 --> 00:04:08.860
(rocks exploding)

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.980
- [Narrator] And then,
there's the surrounding water

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.570
that engineers have to deal with.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:16.110
- As the water is coming
down from the mountainside,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.550
it's moving fast.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.260
- [Narrator] To manage this, Heffron says

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.440
there are various drainage
systems like this or this.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.510
- A lot of times you'll see kind of a pipe

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.130
sticking out from the side of the road,

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.700
and it comes out here and drains

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.980
away from the roadway into the creek.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.090
- [Narrator] A lot of mountain roads

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.560
started out as wagon trails,

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.840
which generally followed
streams and creeks.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.090
This was often the
easiest and cheapest way

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.470
to get through the terrain.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.304
Over time, those wagon
trails were adjusted

00:04:42.304 --> 00:04:44.635
and widened to fit automobiles.

00:04:44.635 --> 00:04:47.340
But being so close to
water means designers

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.772
have to add elements like...

00:04:48.772 --> 00:04:49.710
- Riprap.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.850
Riprap is just big rocks placed in a way

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:58.410
such that it helps stabilize and maintain

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.060
the side of the creek,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.550
which is supporting the
side of your roadway.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.140
- [Narrator] Water mitigation also helps

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.430
prevent soil erosion,

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.797
which can be extremely
dangerous for mountain roads.

00:05:07.797 --> 00:05:09.120
(siren wailing)

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.810
In 2020, a wildfire
burned over 30,000 acres

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.430
in Colorado's Glenwood Canyon.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.200
And because there was no vegetation,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.540
the storms that hit the
area the following year

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.820
caused repeated flooding and debris flow

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.590
that shut down a section of the interstate

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.940
for about two weeks.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.072
- I-70 is closed once
again in Glenwood Canyon,

00:05:26.072 --> 00:05:29.310
this time for five mudslides

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.352
covering hundreds of feet of highway.

00:05:31.352 --> 00:05:33.000
- [Narrator] Even though mountain roads

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.250
often have more variables than flat roads,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.736
Heffron says Consistency
is still the goal.

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:40.680
- We're gonna have some steep turns.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.840
But if all of a sudden in our design

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.840
we interject along the
roadway a very steep turn,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.910
then your safety overall goes down

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.120
because of the lack of consistency.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.490
As people are traveling a mountain road,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.490
you don't want them to think
about the infrastructure.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:03.060
You want them to think about
the beauty of the nature

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.530
that they're traveling through.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.938
You're getting the full
experience. It's snowing.

00:06:06.938 --> 00:06:08.122
(laughs softly)

00:06:08.122 --> 00:06:10.705
(gentle music)

